--- Finding (brilliant_numbers): inside a function, an empty list pushed onto an
--- empty list of lists before the pushed list has items (`groups = push[groups,
--- group]` while `group = []`) leaves the outer list's items of no known kind,
--- `List(List(?))`, even where the same list is later pushed holding ints. The
--- compiled program does not build.
---
--- Interpreted (right):
---     [[], [1]]
--- Compiled:
---     error[E0308]: mismatched types
---       --> src/main.rs:30:49
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused
---     what Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i`
---     runs it meanwhile)
--- Expected: [[], [1]] in both modes.
---
--- `crow build --ir`: `let groups: List(List(?)) = []` beside
--- `let group: List(Int) = []`, though the function answers List(List(Int));
--- the Rust declares `groups: Vec<Vec<String>>` and pushes a Vec<i64> into it.
--- The same lines at the top level type `groups: List(List(Int))` and run in
--- both modes; inside the function they build only when a later ASSIGNMENT
--- (`groups = push[groups, group]`, not `return push[groups, group]`) pushes
--- the list once it holds ints. Found grouping primes by their number of
--- digits (a new group started, the finished one pushed, in one loop).
fn groups_of[n]
    groups = []
    group = []
    groups = push[groups, group]
    group = push[group, n]
    return push[groups, group]

log[groups_of[1]]
